    <div class="definition-block">
      <p>Buying your first home in Australia in 2026 is genuinely achievable — but it requires more planning than most people expect. The median Australian home price crossed $1 million nationally in 2026. That sounds daunting, but government support has also expanded significantly. The <strong>First Home Guarantee now has no income caps and unlimited places</strong>, Help to Buy launched in December 2025 as a new shared equity scheme, and state grants in Queensland reach $30,000 for new builds. With the right strategy, many first home buyers can enter the market with as little as 5% deposit — without paying Lenders Mortgage Insurance (LMI).</p>
    </div>

    <h2>How Much Do You Actually Need to Save?</h2>
    <p>This is the question almost every first home buyer gets wrong. The deposit is not the only cash you need. Many buyers hit their deposit target and then discover at settlement that they are short because they forgot to budget for stamp duty, legal fees, building inspections, and lender charges. A rough rule is to add 5–8% on top of your deposit target to cover all upfront costs.</p>
    <p>The standard deposit benchmark is <strong>20% of the purchase price</strong>. At 20%, your loan-to-value ratio sits at 80%, which means you avoid LMI — a one-off insurance premium that protects the lender (not you) if you default. On a $700,000 purchase, LMI can cost $17,000–$25,000. That said, government schemes now allow eligible first home buyers to purchase with a 5% or even 2% deposit without triggering LMI at all.</p>
    <p>For a $700,000 purchase, here is a realistic breakdown of total upfront costs across four states in 2026, assuming a 5% deposit and first home buyer concessions apply:</p>

