    <p>Always verify current stamp duty thresholds at your state's revenue office, as these figures can change. Use our <a href="/australia/stamp-duty-calculator/">Stamp Duty Calculator</a>, which applies first home buyer concessions automatically for every state and territory. Before you settle on a savings target, check your borrowing power — use our <a href="/australia/borrowing-power-calculator/">Borrowing Power Calculator</a> to see your maximum loan based on your income and expenses, including APRA's standard 3% serviceability buffer.</p>

    <h2>Every First Home Buyer Scheme Available in 2026</h2>
    <p>Australia's landscape of first home buyer support changed substantially in late 2025. Here is a clear breakdown of every federal scheme currently active.</p>

    <h3>First Home Guarantee — 5% Deposit, No LMI</h3>
    <p>The First Home Guarantee — administered by <a href="https://www.housingaustralia.gov.au/support-buy-home/first-home-guarantee" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Housing Australia</a> — allows eligible first home buyers to purchase with a 5% deposit without paying LMI. The federal government guarantees up to 15% of the loan to the lender, so you still own 100% of your home from day one.</p>
    <p>The scheme was significantly expanded from 1 October 2025. Income caps (previously $125,000 for singles, $200,000 for couples) have been <strong>removed entirely</strong>. There are now <strong>unlimited places</strong> — no more annual waitlists or place limits. Property price caps have also been lifted, with Sydney's cap now at $1,500,000, Melbourne at $950,000, and Brisbane at $1,000,000.</p>
    <p>You apply through a participating lender — you cannot apply directly to Housing Australia. The panel of approved lenders includes major banks such as CBA, NAB, and Westpac, as well as a range of smaller banks and non-bank lenders.</p>

    <h3>Family Home Guarantee — 2% Deposit for Single Parents</h3>
    <p>Single parents or single legal guardians with at least one dependent child can purchase with a 2% deposit and no LMI under the Family Home Guarantee. The government guarantees up to 18% of the loan value. An income cap of $125,000 applies. This scheme recognises that single-income households face fundamentally different savings challenges.</p>

    <h3>Help to Buy — New Shared Equity Scheme, Launched December 2025</h3>
    <p>Help to Buy is Australia's brand new federal shared equity scheme. Under this model, the government co-purchases the property with you, contributing up to <strong>40% of the purchase price for new builds</strong> and up to <strong>30% for existing homes</strong>. In return, the government holds an equivalent equity stake. When you sell or refinance, you repay the government's proportionate share — including any capital growth.</p>
    <p>You can enter with just a 2% deposit and a significantly smaller loan. Income caps apply: singles must earn under $100,000 per year and couples under $160,000 combined. There are 10,000 places per year, allocated on a first-come, first-served basis. This scheme is ideal for lower-income buyers who want the smallest possible monthly repayment. The trade-off is clear: you share any future property gains with the government. It cannot be combined with the First Home Guarantee — you must choose one.</p>

    <h3>First Home Super Saver (FHSS) Scheme</h3>
    <p>The FHSS scheme allows you to make voluntary contributions to your superannuation fund and later withdraw them — plus associated earnings — for your first home deposit. You can contribute up to $15,000 per financial year and withdraw up to $50,000 in total. For couples, this doubles to $100,000 combined.</p>
    <p>The tax advantage is meaningful. Concessional (before-tax) contributions into super are taxed at just 15%, which is typically far below your marginal income tax rate. On a $90,000 salary, saving $15,000 a year through the FHSS scheme saves approximately $5,000–$7,000 in tax compared with saving in a standard bank account — every single year you contribute.</p>
    <p>There are important timing rules. You must obtain an FHSS <strong>determination from the <a href="https://www.ato.gov.au/individuals-and-families/super-for-individuals-and-families/super/withdrawing-and-using-your-super/early-access-to-super/first-home-super-saver-scheme" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">ATO</a> before signing your property contract</strong>. The release request then takes 15–25 business days to process. You only get one FHSS release in your lifetime, so plan carefully. The FHSS can be combined with the First Home Guarantee, meaning you can build your deposit inside super tax-effectively, then use it alongside the 5% deposit guarantee when you are ready to buy.</p>

    <h3>State-Based Grants (First Home Owner Grant)</h3>
    <p>Most states offer a First Home Owner Grant (FHOG) for new or substantially renovated properties. Note that state grants generally apply only to <strong>new homes or substantial renovations</strong>, not established properties. Stamp duty concessions, however, often cover both new and existing properties depending on the state.</p>

