    <h2>How to Save Your Deposit Faster</h2>
    <p><strong>Use the FHSS scheme from day one.</strong> If you are more than 12 months from buying, start making voluntary super contributions now. Every dollar you contribute saves you tax on the way in. The sooner you start, the larger your tax saving.</p>
    <p><strong>Open a high-interest savings account.</strong> As of mid-2026, the top savings rates in Australia are between 5.0% and 5.5% per annum. On a $50,000 balance, that generates $2,500–$2,750 per year in interest — meaningful progress toward your target.</p>
    <p><strong>Run a 50/30/20 budget.</strong> Allocate 50% of take-home income to essentials, 30% to discretionary spending, and 20% or more directly to your deposit savings account. Use our <a href="/australia/budget-calculator/">Budget Calculator</a> to map your current spending against the 50/30/20 framework and identify where you can redirect more toward savings.</p>
    <p><strong>Reduce your taxable income legally.</strong> Salary sacrificing additional amounts into superannuation lowers your taxable income, which means lower PAYG withholding from your employer and more after-tax cash. See our <a href="/australia/salary-sacrifice-calculator/">Salary Sacrifice Calculator</a> to model the exact dollar saving.</p>
    <p><strong>Know your savings timeline.</strong> Use our <a href="/australia/savings-goal-calculator/">Savings Goal Calculator</a> to enter your target amount, current balance, monthly savings, and interest rate — and get a clear month-by-month projection.</p>

    <h2>Getting Pre-Approval: What Lenders Actually Check</h2>
    <p>Pre-approval is a written confirmation from a lender of how much they are prepared to lend you, subject to final property and income verification. It is not a guaranteed loan offer — but it is an essential tool before you start house-hunting.</p>
    <p>To assess your pre-approval, lenders evaluate your gross income, regular living expenses, any existing debts (including HECS-HELP balances, which reduce borrowing capacity), credit card limits, employment stability, and credit score. You will typically need to provide your last three payslips, three to six months of bank statements, identification documents, and statements for all existing financial commitments.</p>
    <p>Pre-approval typically remains valid for 90 days. Critically, do not make any significant financial changes during this period. Taking out a new car loan, applying for additional credit cards, or changing jobs can invalidate your pre-approval — even after you have exchanged contracts.</p>
    <p>One important note on HECS-HELP debt: lenders assess HECS repayments as a monthly obligation. A $50,000 HECS balance can reduce your borrowing capacity by $30,000–$50,000 depending on the lender. Use our <a href="/australia/hecs-repayment-calculator/">HECS Repayment Calculator</a> to understand your current annual repayment and how it affects your financial position.</p>

    <h2>The Buying Process: Private Treaty vs Auction</h2>

    <h3>Buying via Private Treaty</h3>
    <p>The vendor sets an asking price. You make an offer and negotiate until both parties agree. Once the vendor accepts, you exchange contracts. In most states, a cooling-off period applies: 5 business days in NSW and QLD, 3 days in Victoria. During cooling-off, you can withdraw from the purchase, though a small penalty (typically 0.25% of the purchase price) may apply. Use this window to complete your building and pest inspection and have your solicitor review the contract. Once the cooling-off period expires and any conditions are met, the contract becomes unconditional and settlement is scheduled — usually 30–90 days after exchange.</p>

    <h3>Buying at Auction</h3>
    <p>At auction, there is no cooling-off period. If you are the highest bidder and the property sells above the reserve price, you are <strong>immediately and unconditionally committed</strong> to the purchase. You sign the contract on the day and pay the deposit — typically 10% — on the spot. This means all your due diligence must be completed before the auction: unconditional pre-approval, a reviewed contract, and a completed building and pest inspection. Never bid at auction without having done all three. A failed settlement after a successful auction bid can result in forfeiting your entire 10% deposit.</p>

    <h2>Settlement: What Happens on the Day</h2>
    <p>Settlement is the legal transfer of property ownership from the vendor to you. Your conveyancer coordinates the exchange of funds and title documents with the vendor's conveyancer. In 2026, almost all Australian settlements are conducted electronically through the PEXA platform.</p>
    <p>On settlement day, your lender releases the loan funds, the vendor's mortgage is discharged, the title is transferred into your name, and your conveyancer confirms completion. After that, the real estate agent releases the keys. Your conveyancer will also conduct a final title search before settlement to confirm there are no outstanding caveats, orders, or encumbrances on the property.</p>

    <h2>Upfront Costs That First Home Buyers Frequently Miss</h2>
    <p><strong>Building and pest inspection ($400–$700).</strong> Non-negotiable for any established property. A professional inspection identifies structural defects, water damage, termite activity, and safety issues that are not visible to the naked eye. A single missed defect can cost $30,000 or more to rectify.</p>
    <p><strong>Conveyancing fees ($900–$2,500).</strong> Your conveyancer handles the contract review, title searches, liaising with your lender, and coordinating settlement. The fee varies by state and complexity. Do not try to save money here — a conveyancing error can cost far more than the fee itself.</p>
    <p><strong>Lender fees ($0–$700).</strong> Many lenders charge an application fee, valuation fee, or settlement fee. Some lenders waive these for first home buyers under government guarantee schemes. Ask explicitly before selecting a lender.</p>
    <p><strong>Moving costs ($500–$3,000+).</strong> Professional movers for a two-bedroom apartment typically cost $800–$1,500. A four-bedroom house with interstate contents can reach $3,000–$5,000. Budget for this separately.</p>
    <p><strong>Immediate repairs or appliances.</strong> Even in a well-maintained property, first home buyers commonly spend $2,000–$8,000 in the first three months on items the vendor left behind or wore out before settlement — hot water systems, blinds, appliances, and garden maintenance.</p>

    <h2>Worked Example: Sophie's Journey From Saving to Settlement</h2>
    <p>Real numbers make the process far easier to understand. Here is how a typical first home buyer in Brisbane — let's call her Sophie — navigates the entire journey in 2026.</p>
    <p><strong>Sophie's starting position.</strong> Sophie is 29, earns $88,000 per year as a project coordinator, and has $18,000 in savings. She has a $32,000 HECS-HELP debt. She wants to buy a two-bedroom unit in Brisbane's inner suburbs, targeting a purchase price of $650,000.</p>

    <h3>Step 1: Setting the savings target</h3>
    <p>Sophie's 5% deposit on a $650,000 property is $32,500. Queensland waives stamp duty for first home buyers on properties under $700,000, so her stamp duty cost is $0. Adding conveyancing ($1,200), a building inspection ($550), and a small lender fee buffer ($500), her total upfront cash requirement is approximately $34,750. She already has $18,000, meaning she needs to save a further $16,750.</p>

    <h3>Step 2: Using the FHSS to save tax</h3>
    <p>Sophie starts salary sacrificing $1,000 per month into her super fund as concessional contributions. At her marginal tax rate of 32.5%, she would normally pay $325 in tax on each $1,000 earned. Inside super, it is taxed at just 15% — saving her $175 per month, or $2,100 per year. Over 14 months, she accumulates $14,000 in eligible FHSS contributions plus associated earnings, bringing her total FHSS release amount to approximately $13,800 after the 30% tax offset on withdrawal. She keeps her remaining savings in a high-interest account earning 5.2% per annum. After 14 months of also depositing $900 per month into savings, she reaches her $34,750 target.</p>

    <h3>Step 3: Getting pre-approval</h3>
    <p>Sophie applies for pre-approval through a participating First Home Guarantee lender. The lender calculates her borrowing capacity using her $88,000 income, minus her HECS-HELP repayment obligation of $4,928 per year (5.6% of income above the 2026–27 threshold), and her estimated living expenses. After APRA's 3% serviceability buffer is applied, Sophie receives pre-approval for a maximum loan of $530,000 — enough to comfortably purchase at $650,000 with a 5% deposit.</p>

    <h3>Step 4: Applying for the First Home Guarantee</h3>
    <p>Because Sophie is a first home buyer purchasing with a 5% deposit in Brisbane (price cap: $1,000,000), she qualifies for the First Home Guarantee. There are no income caps in 2026, so her $88,000 salary is not a barrier. The government guarantees 15% of her loan — $97,500 — which means she pays zero LMI on the $617,500 loan. At typical LMI rates for her LVR, this saves her approximately $18,000 that would otherwise have been added to her loan balance. She also checks Queensland's $30,000 First Home Owner Grant — but the FHOG applies only to new homes, and Sophie is buying an established unit, so she does not qualify. She still benefits from the full stamp duty exemption and the LMI saving.</p>

    <h3>Step 5: The purchase and settlement</h3>
    <p>Sophie finds a two-bedroom unit listed at $645,000 via private treaty. She makes an offer of $638,000, which the vendor accepts. Contracts are exchanged with a 5-business-day cooling-off period. Her building inspector identifies minor issues only (cost: $530). Her conveyancer finds no unusual contract conditions. She waives cooling-off and the contract becomes unconditional. Settlement is set for 45 days after exchange. On settlement day, her lender releases the $605,500 loan, the vendor's mortgage is discharged, and title transfers electronically through PEXA. Sophie's conveyancer confirms completion at 2:14 pm. The agent releases the keys an hour later.</p>

