    <h2>The 5 Most Expensive First Home Buyer Mistakes</h2>
    <p><strong>Bidding at auction without unconditional pre-approval.</strong> If your finance falls through after a successful auction bid, you lose your 10% deposit. Always have fully unconditional pre-approval — not just conditional — before you raise your hand.</p>
    <p><strong>Buying just over a stamp duty threshold.</strong> In Victoria, purchasing at $601,000 instead of $599,000 triggers a concession rather than a full exemption — costing up to $31,000 in additional stamp duty. Check the exact thresholds for your state before setting your maximum budget.</p>
    <p><strong>Missing the FHSS timing requirement.</strong> You must obtain an FHSS determination from the ATO before signing your property contract. The release request takes 15–25 business days. Many buyers forget this and miss the scheme entirely.</p>
    <p><strong>Skipping the building inspection.</strong> It is legal to purchase a property without a building inspection. It is also potentially catastrophic. Never skip it — even for a brand-new apartment in a strata scheme.</p>
    <p><strong>Maxing out your borrowing capacity with no buffer.</strong> Borrowing the maximum your lender will offer leaves you exposed to rate rises, job changes, or unexpected expenses. A buffer of at least three to six months of mortgage repayments in a savings or offset account is the minimum safety margin worth maintaining. See our <a href="/australia/offset-account-calculator/">Offset Account Calculator</a> to see how an offset account reduces your interest.</p>

    <h2>How to Use This Guide: Your Step-by-Step Action Plan</h2>
    <p>Reading about buying a home and actually moving through the process are two different things. Here is a practical sequence of actions you can take right now, in order, regardless of where you are in your savings journey.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Know your numbers before anything else.</strong> Use <a href="/australia/first-home-buyer-calculator/">CalcPhi's First Home Buyer Calculator</a> to model your stamp duty, upfront costs, and deposit requirements for your target price and state. This gives you a single concrete savings target. Do this today — it takes two minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>Check your borrowing power.</strong> Use <a href="/australia/borrowing-power-calculator/">CalcPhi's Borrowing Power Calculator</a> to understand the maximum loan you qualify for at your current income and expenses, including APRA's 3% serviceability buffer. This tells you the property price range worth saving toward.</li>
      <li><strong>Open your FHSS contribution immediately.</strong> If you are more than 12 months from purchasing, contact your super fund and start making voluntary concessional contributions now. Use <a href="/australia/salary-sacrifice-calculator/">CalcPhi's Salary Sacrifice Calculator</a> to find the right contribution level for your budget.</li>
      <li><strong>Open a dedicated high-interest savings account.</strong> Keep your deposit savings completely separate from your everyday banking. Set up an automatic transfer on the day your salary lands. Target an account paying 5.0%+ per annum. Use <a href="/australia/savings-goal-calculator/">CalcPhi's Savings Goal Calculator</a> to track exactly when you will hit your target.</li>
      <li><strong>Check stamp duty thresholds for your target state.</strong> Use <a href="/australia/stamp-duty-calculator/">CalcPhi's Stamp Duty Calculator</a> to understand the exact duty payable and the price cap below which you receive a full exemption. Build this threshold into your maximum purchase price before you start house-hunting.</li>
      <li><strong>Get your documents organised early.</strong> Lenders request the same set of documents for every pre-approval: three recent payslips, three to six months of bank statements, identification documents, existing loan statements, and HECS balance confirmation. Organising these in advance means you can move quickly when you are ready.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply for pre-approval through a First Home Guarantee lender.</strong> When your savings are 4–8 weeks from target, apply through a lender on the Housing Australia participating lender panel and confirm your eligibility for the First Home Guarantee. Pre-approval is valid for 90 days — apply when you are genuinely ready to start making offers.</li>
      <li><strong>Engage a conveyancer before you exchange contracts.</strong> Research and shortlist a conveyancer in your state while you are still saving — so you have a name ready to call the moment you receive an accepted offer or want a pre-auction contract reviewed.</li>
      <li><strong>Complete building and pest inspection before committing.</strong> For private treaty purchases, do this during the cooling-off period. For auctions, do this before bidding. Never skip it, regardless of how competitive the market feels.</li>
      <li><strong>Prepare for settlement costs beyond the deposit.</strong> In the final weeks before settlement, confirm you have funds available for conveyancing fees, lender fees, and moving costs. These are paid at or around settlement and are separate from your deposit. Running short at this stage — after exchange — is an avoidable and stressful position to be in.</li>
    </ol>

