    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <ul class="faq-list">
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Can I use the First Home Guarantee if I earn a high income in 2026?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Yes. As of 1 October 2025, income caps were removed from the First Home Guarantee entirely. Any Australian citizen or permanent resident purchasing their first home can now apply regardless of income, provided the property is within the applicable price cap for their location. There are also now unlimited places, so there is no waiting for a spot to become available.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Can I use my superannuation to help buy my first home?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Yes, through the First Home Super Saver (FHSS) scheme. You can contribute voluntarily to your super fund — up to $15,000 per financial year — and withdraw a total of up to $50,000 (or $100,000 for couples) for your first home deposit. The tax benefit is significant: concessional contributions are taxed at just 15%, which is often much lower than your marginal income tax rate. You must apply for an FHSS determination from the ATO before signing any property contract, and the release takes 15–25 business days.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">What is LMI and how do I avoid it?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Lenders Mortgage Insurance (LMI) is a one-off insurance premium that protects the lender — not you — if you default on a home loan with a deposit below 20%. On a $700,000 property with a 5% deposit, LMI can cost $17,000–$25,000. You can avoid it by saving a 20% deposit, by using the First Home Guarantee (5% deposit, no LMI), by using the Family Home Guarantee if you are a single parent (2% deposit, no LMI), or by meeting certain professional exemptions offered by some lenders.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">How long does settlement take after exchanging contracts?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Most Australian property settlements occur 30–90 days after exchange of contracts, though this can be negotiated. A 30-day settlement is relatively fast and requires your lender to move quickly — 45–60 days is more common. If you are buying at auction, the settlement timeframe is typically stated in the contract of sale and is non-negotiable.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Do I need a conveyancer or a solicitor?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Both can handle property settlement. A licensed conveyancer specialises specifically in property transfers — they handle the contract review, title searches, and settlement coordination. A property solicitor does all of this and can also provide legal advice if complications arise. For a straightforward residential purchase, a conveyancer is usually sufficient and often less expensive. For properties with complex title issues, off-the-plan purchases, or unusual contract conditions, a property solicitor adds extra protection.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Does HECS-HELP debt affect my ability to get a home loan?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Yes, it reduces your borrowing capacity. Lenders treat HECS repayments as a monthly expense regardless of your actual income-based repayment. A $50,000 HECS balance can reduce your maximum loan by $30,000–$50,000 depending on the lender's methodology.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

